#!/usr/bin/env php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

/**
 * `scout` — the CLI entry point (spec/PROJECT_BRIEF.md §10).
 *
 * Deliberately thin: everything testable lives in `Scout\Cli\Scout`, which takes its streams as
 * arguments so a test can assert on REAL stdout rather than on a description of it.
 */

// NO ARGUMENT REACHES A STACK TRACE FROM THIS PROCESS (C2 round 3, 2026-09-04).
//
// PHP ships `zend.exception_ignore_args = Off`, so an uncaught trace prints the first 15 characters
// of every string ARGUMENT of every live frame — this codebase's and the built-ins' alike. Three
// surfaces were repaired one at a time (`tools/dump-eml.php`, `ImapMailbox::login()`,
// `SmtpTransport::writeCredential()`) and a review panel then found a FOURTH that no per-site fix
// had reached: the CONSTRUCTOR. Each parameter carries its own 15-character budget there, so the
// username no longer spends it first and the password arrives in CLEAR TEXT, measured:
//
//     #0 …: Scout\Adapters\Mail\ImapMailbox->__construct('imap.example.ne...',
//        'annette-the-sub...', 'SuperSecretPass...', 'INBOX', '993')
//
// Fifteen characters, against the two that made the IMAP call site a finding. It is latent — three
// `(int)` casts on the port keep the binding from raising — but "latent behind a cast nobody
// asserts" is this repo's own definition of luck rather than a guard.
//
// **This line is the structural answer, and it is here rather than at a fourth call site because
// the per-site pattern had already been shown to miss one.** It covers every surface at once,
// including the ones nobody has enumerated. The per-site fixes stay: they hold for code reached
// outside this executable — `tools/`, the test suite, a future entrypoint — which this line cannot.
//
// **Stated cost: traces from the deployed watcher lose ALL argument information**, which is real
// debugging value given up. It is the right trade here because this project already redacts its
// diagnostics on purpose (`Core\Redact`, `MailboxError`, `ChannelError::secrets()`), and because a
// credential in a container log is not recoverable once written.
ini_set('zend.exception_ignore_args', '1');

$autoload = __DIR__ . '/../vendor/autoload.php';

if (!is_file($autoload)) {
    fwrite(STDERR, "vendor/autoload.php est absent — lancez `composer install`.\n");
    exit(2);
}

require $autoload;

// `.env` is loaded HERE rather than inside `Scout`, and the placement is load-bearing. The test
// suite constructs `Scout` directly against the real repo root, so a load inside the class would
// pull the developer's actual credentials into every test run — a suite that mails somebody, or
// polls their mailbox, because it instantiated a CLI. The executable is the one place that is never
// a test subject.
//
// Before this existed, `.env` was inert outside Docker and the documented workaround was
// `set -a; . ./.env; set +a` — the SHELL, which executes the file rather than reading it. See
// `Scout\Config\DotEnv` for what that cost on 2026-08-22. The real environment still wins over
// the file, so `RENT_SCOUT_DB=/tmp/throwaway bin/scout run` behaves as it always did.
try {
    Scout\Config\DotEnv::load(dirname(__DIR__) . '/.env');
} catch (Scout\Config\ConfigError $e) {
    // Not routed through Scout's handler: this runs before one exists, and a `.env` the parser
    // refuses is a startup refusal in the most literal sense. The message names a line NUMBER and
    // never the line, because the file holds credentials.
    fwrite(STDERR, 'configuration : ' . $e->getMessage() . "\n");
    exit(2);
}

// Ruled 2026-08-07 (Q34): a Docker container without TZ runs UTC, so "08:00 local" silently becomes
// 10:00 Paris in summer. Read from the environment, with the same default `.env.example` documents.
$tz = getenv('TZ');
date_default_timezone_set(is_string($tz) && $tz !== '' ? $tz : 'Europe/Paris');

exit((new Scout\Cli\Scout(dirname(__DIR__)))->run(array_slice($argv, 1)));
